.TH CAU 1 "2026-11-02" "CAU EDR" "CAU EDR User Commands"
.SH NAME
cau \- transparent, open-source malware detection engine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cau
.I command
.RI [ flags ]
.RI [ path ...]
.br
.B cau completion
.RI < bash | zsh | fish >
.br
.B cau version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B CAU EDR
is a malware detection and triage engine written in Go. Detectors read every
file once: hash signatures, YARA rules, string and API heuristics, a static
structural pass (entropy, packer detection, section and entry-point anomalies,
TLSH/ssdeep/imphash similarity), a steganography pass (zero-width and other
invisible codepoints in text and code), and a behavioural tradecraft pass that
asks what a file does rather than what it is called \(em web shells, network
shells, in-memory loaders, LOLBAS and GTFOBins abuse, trusted-SaaS command
channels (the lolol.farm catalogue) and EDR-bypass primitives (ArcticFox
detection-bypass catalogue). Document, archive, CHM, ISO and Linux software
package passes read structured files with the same contract.
.PP
Every finding is explained with a labeled class and a MITRE ATT&CK technique,
and every report is machine-readable with CI-friendly exit codes. A fusion
lattice joins the detectors' evidence, so sub-threshold sources that corroborate
each other are reported as SUSPICIOUS with the sources that agreed named.
.PP
Detections do not trust code-signing certificates. A file signed by a known
key is still scanned on its content; the signature is reported, not believed.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B cau scan \fR[\fPflags\fR] \fP[\fBpath\fP ...\fR]
Scan files and directories recursively. Flags select detectors, thresholds,
output and failure behaviour.
.TP
.B cau hash \fR[\fPflags\fR] \fP<\fBpath\fP ...\fR\fP
Print the SHA-256, imphash, TLSH and ssdeep digests of a file.
.TP
.B cau cluster \fR[\fPflags\fR] \fP<\fBpath\fP ...\fR\fP
Group PE files by import-table similarity.
.TP
.B cau add-hash \fR[\fPflags\fR]
Append a hash signature to the database.
.TP
.B cau add-fuzzy \fR[\fPflags\fR]
Append a TLSH, ssdeep or imphash signature.
.TP
.B cau eval \fR<\fBvalidate\fP|\fBrun\fP|\fBreport\fP|\fBdiff\fP|\fBgate\fP|\fBpolicy\fP\fR>
Corpus evaluation harness: validate a manifest, run it, report on it, diff two
runs and gate a run against a measured baseline.
.TP
.B cau heldout \fR<\fBinit\fP|\fBadd\fP|\fBpull\fP|\fBsync\fP|\fBremove\fP|\fBrm\fP|\fBlist\fP|\fBmanifest\fP\fR>
Manage the encrypted held-out recall-corpus hash database.
.TP
.B cau rules \fR<\fBlint\fP|\fBlist\fR\fP\fR
Lint the YARA rules, or list them with their metadata.
.TP
.B cau coverage \fR[\fPflags\fR]
ATT&CK coverage matrix of the shipped content, as text, JSON, SARIF or an ATT&CK
Navigator layer; \fB\-\-expect\fP gates the content against a recorded technique list.
.TP
.B cau quarantine \fR<\fBadd\fP|\fBlist\fP|\fBrestore\fP|\fBverify\fP|\fBdelete\fR\fP\fR
Vault flagged files in a protected store and restore them deliberately.
.TP
.B cau canary \fR<\fBplant\fP|\fBcheck\fP\fR\fP\fR
Plant decoy files and detect tampering with them.
.TP
.B cau peel \fR[\fPflags\fR] \fP<\fBfile\fR\fP...\fR
Walk an obfuscation chain, reporting depth as a signal in its own right.
.TP
.B cau firmware \fR[\fPflags\fR] \fP<\fBimage\fR\fP\fR
Parse a UEFI firmware image: firmware-volume and file inventory, integrity and
an implant blocklist.
.TP
.B cau boot\-scan \fR[\fPflags\fR]
Read the boot chain, strictly read-only: the MBR, GPT and partition boot
sectors of disks named with \fB\-disk\fR, the EFI System Partition, Secure
Boot state and boot entries, the WPBT table and PCI option ROMs.
.TP
.B cau ps \fR[\fPflags\fR]
Scan the live process table for fileless, masquerade, injection and network
shell indicators; \fB\-\-respond\fR stops or kills confirmed processes.
.TP
.B cau net \fR[\fPflags\fR] | \fR<\fBblock\fP|\fBunblock\fP|\fBblocks\fP|\fBclear\fR\fP\fR
Scan the network plane, read entirely from /proc and /etc: every socket the
kernel holds, per network namespace, attributed to the process and the
executable behind it, plus the resolver configuration and the neighbour table.
Reports fileless egress and bind shells, known-bad peers from the abuse-intel
feed, handler ports, DNS-plane escape, connect sweeps, beaconing cadence and
default-gateway MAC churn. \fB\-\-respond block\fR drops a confirmed finding's
peers in cau's own nftables table or iptables chains; the block expires
(\fB\-\-block\-ttl\fR, 1h by default) and never covers loopback, this host's own
addresses, its gateways or its resolvers. The subcommands manage blocks by hand;
\fBclear\fR removes exactly what cau created.
.TP
.B cau defend \fR[\fPflags\fR]
Chain every detection layer: correlate process, file, network, persistence,
kernel, canary and package findings into incidents, take in telemetry from other
hosts and sensors with \fB\-\-ingest\fR, scan new files as they land, and respond
to confirmed attacks with \fB\-\-respond\fR, \fB\-\-quarantine\fR and
\fB\-\-netblock\fR.
.TP
.B cau persist \fR[\fPflags\fR]
Audit the host for persistence: preload, cron, systemd, rc files, autostart,
SSH keys, accounts, sudoers, PAM and set-uid programs in writable directories.
.TP
.B cau installed \fR[\fPflags\fR]
Audit installed-package maintainer scripts (dpkg and pacman) for root-level
tradecraft.
.TP
.B cau feed \fR<\fBlist\fP|\fBcheck\fP|\fBupdate\fR\fP\fR
Manage the local abuse-intel feed from its offline cache.
.TP
.B cau intake \fB\-\-format \fP<\fPfmt\fP>\fP \fR[\fPflags\fR]
Ingest a threat-intel feed into the signature database.
.TP
.B cau update \fB\-\-from\fP|\fB\-\-url\fP \fR[\fPflags\fR]
Install a signed signature-database bundle; fail-closed on a bad signature.
.TP
.B cau selftest \fR[\fB\-\-record\fP\fR] \fR[\fPflags\fR]
Verify the bundled detection content has not been tampered with.
.TP
.B cau verify\-self \fB\-\-from\fP|\fB\-\-url\fP \fR[\fPflags\fR]
Verify this binary against the signed release manifest.
.TP
.B cau bundle \fR<\fBupdate\fP|\fBchecksums\fR\fR\fR
Produce signed release artifacts; intended for CI.
.TP
.B cau completion \fR<\fBshell\fP\fR\fP\fR
Print the shell-completion script for \fBbash\fP, \fBzsh\fP or \fBfish\fP.
.TP
.B cau version
Print the engine version.
.SH OPTIONS
The options carried by \fBcau scan\fP, the command most often used:
.TP
.BR \-file " " \fIfile\fR
Scan a single file (positional arguments are accepted too).
.TP
.BR \-dir " " \fIdirectory\fR
Scan a directory recursively.
.TP
.BR \-db " " "\fIfile\fR"
The hash signature database. When omitted it is auto-discovered.
.TP
.BR \-rules " " "\fIdirectory\fR"
The YARA rules directory.
.TP
.BR \-format " " "\fBtext\fP|\fBjson\fP|\fBsarif\fP|\fBocsf\fR"
Output format. Machine formats never write the banner to stdout, so stdout is
always valid for a parser. The SIEM sink formats \fBecs\fR, \fBcef\fR,
\fBleef\fR, \fBgelf\fR, \fBsyslog\fR, \fBsyslog\-ecs\fR, \fBsyslog\-cef\fR,
\fBsyslog\-leef\fR and \fBotlp\fR carry the same findings as \fBocsf\fR.
.TP
.BR \-sink\-url " " "\fIurl\fR"
Forward the findings to a collector after the scan: \fBudp://\fR,
\fBtcp://\fR, \fBtls://\fR\fIhost:port\fR or \fBhttps://\fR\fIhost/path\fR.
\fB\-sink\-format\fR picks the sink format sent. Delivery is best-effort and
never changes the exit code.
.TP
.BR \-fail\-on " " "\fBclean\fP|\fBheuristic\fP|\fBmalicious\fP|\fBnone\fR
The findings tier that makes the run exit non-zero.
.TP
.BR \-jobs " " \fIn\fR
Parallel workers.
.TP
.BR \-interval " " \fIduration\fR
Daemon mode: rescan every interval (for example \fB15m\fR) until a termination
signal arrives.
.TP
.BR \-pretty\fR, \fB\-quiet\fR
Pretty-print machine formats, or suppress the banner and progress.
.TP
.B \-no\-yara\fR, \fB\-no\-heur\fR, \fB\-no\-static\fR, \fB\-no\-fuzzy\fR, \fB\-no\-stego\fR, \fB\-no\-fusion\fR
Disable a single detector. Disabled detectors are named in the report rather
than silently omitted.
.PP
Each detector has a matching threshold flag, for example
\fB\-heuristic\-threshold\fR and \fB\-stego\-threshold\fR, that sets the score at
which the tier is reported. \fBcau scan \-h\fR lists them all.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The run completed and nothing at or above \fB\-\-fail\-on\fR was found.
.TP
.B 1
A finding at or above \fB\-\-fail\-on\fR was reported.
.TP
.B 2
The run completed but some inputs could not be scanned. The package manager and
CI use this for a partial scan.
.TP
.B 3
The run itself failed: a missing signature database, an unusable input, a
timeout.
.TP
.B 4
The command line was invalid.
.SH RESOURCES
The signature database, YARA rules and fuzzy database are located in this order,
the first that exists winning:
.RS
.IP 1. 3
the matching command-line flag,
.IP 2. 3
the matching environment variable
.RB ( CAU_SIGNATURES ,
.BR CAU_RULES ,
.BR CAU_FUZZY ),
.IP 3. 3
next to the binary,
.IP 4. 3
the share directory relative to the binary, which is how a package install
under \fB/usr\fP is found,
.IP 5. 3
\fB/usr/local/share/cau\fP and \fB/usr/share/cau\fP,
.IP 6. 3
the current working directory.
.RE
.PP
When nothing is found, the error lists every path that was tried. A missing
signature database is fatal; missing rules or a missing fuzzy database disable
that detector with a message on stderr, because advertising a feature that
silently did not run is worse than reporting it off.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/cau/rules/*.yar
Bundled YARA rules.
.TP
.I /usr/share/cau/signatures/hashes.txt
Bundled hash signature database.
.TP
.I /usr/share/cau/signatures/fuzzy.txt
Bundled fuzzy-hash database.
.TP
.I /usr/bin/cau\fR, \fI/usr/bin/cau-mcp
The scanner and the Model Context Protocol server.
.TP
.I /usr/share/man/man1/cau.1
This manual page.
.SH EXAMPLES
Scan one file and exit non-zero on a malicious verdict:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cau scan --file suspicious.exe
.fi
.RE
.PP
Scan a tree, emitting SARIF for CI, and fail only on malicious findings:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cau scan --dir ./build --format sarif --output cau.sarif --fail-on malicious
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run as a scheduled service; the unit ships with the package and is not enabled
by default:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo install -Dm0644 cau-scan.timer /etc/systemd/system/
sudo systemctl enable --now cau-scan.timer
.fi
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CAU_SIGNATURES
Path to the hash signature database.
.TP
.B CAU_RULES
Path to the YARA rules directory.
.TP
.B CAU_FUZZY
Path to the fuzzy-hash database.
.TP
.B CAU_SIGNERS
Path to the known-abused-signer blocklist.
